    {
      "slug": "david-with-the-head-of-goliath-castagno",
      "title": "David with the Head of Goliath (Castagno)",
      "year": 1450,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Andrea_del_Castagno_002.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 4152,
      "imageHeight": 6319,
      "story": "David with the Head of Goliath is a painting on a parade shield of leather and wood by the Italian Renaissance painter Andrea del Castagno, created around 1450–1457. It measures 115.5 x 76.5 and 40.6 centimeters. It is now in the collection of the National Gallery of Art, Washington, D.C.",
      "facts": [
        "The painting decorates an ornamental parade shield, curved at the edges, and not designed for actual fighting.",
        "These were often painted, but very rarely with a single figure, especially a Biblical one (David slaying Goliath) rather than a coat of arms, or a more ornamental design."
      ],
