    {
      "slug": "assumption-of-the-virgin-between-st-minias-and-st-julian",
      "title": "Assumption of the Virgin Between St Minias and St Julian",
      "year": 1449,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/33/Del_Castagno_Andrea_Our_Lady_of_the_Assumption_with_Sts_Miniato_and_Julian.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 2704,
      "imageHeight": 2335,
      "story": "The Assumption of the Virgin Between St Minias and St Julian is a painting by the Italian early Renaissance master Andrea del Castagno, executed around 1449–1450. It is now housed in the Gemäldegalerie of Berlin.",
      "facts": [
        "An inscription mentioned by Filippo Baldinucci, dated 20 November 1449, reported that the altarpiece was commissioned by Leonardo di Francesco di Nardo Falladanzi, the rector of San Miniato tra le Torri, a church in Florence.",
        "The church was demolished around 1888 (it was located where the main post office is today), and its heritage was dispersed, after which Andrea del Castagno's Assumption was acquired by Berlin's Gemäldegalerie.",
        "Both men are elegantly dressed in the most expensive of contemporary costumes."
      ],
